There may be research finding average sex differences in some traits or in how couples divide planning and emotional labor. But “men’s brains are literally wired to prioritize themselves over relationship maintenance” is much stronger than what psychology research can usually establish.

A single article may be:
- describing an average difference, not every man;
- measuring a narrow task or self-report scale, not “selfishness” overall;
- influenced by socialization, workload, power, and expectations;
- presented in simplified language by a headline or popular summary.

Even where an average pattern is real, it does not mean a partner has no agency or that you should accept repeated disregard. Remembering an anniversary and responding constructively when hurt are learnable, concrete behaviors. A calendar reminder, shared planning, and a non-defensive apology do not require changing someone’s brain.

If you have the article or its title, I can help look at what it actually found. But you do not need scientific proof to justify your feelings or your standards: if his behavior repeatedly leaves you feeling unimportant and he will not work on it, that is enough to take seriously.

The practical difference is in what you do with new people and what you tolerate once you have evidence.

If you treat it as “men are just like this,” you may:
- excuse repeated selfishness because it seems unavoidable;
- stay longer hoping this is simply the best available;
- stop believing care is possible or meaningful.

If you treat it as a common risk pattern, you can be cautious **without accepting it**:
- don’t assume promises mean much before consistent follow-through;
- look early for shared planning, consideration, and accountability;
- take defensiveness seriously;
- leave when the pattern appears instead of trying to normalize it.

You do not have to give every man unlimited trust, emotional labor, or benefit of the doubt. “Common pattern” can justify a high bar and fast exit. It just does not require you to make poor treatment the baseline you resign yourself to.
